  "abstract": "We prove that every nonempty private vertex set whose external neighbors lie among four vertices has order at least nine in a minimum-order biplanar graph that is not nine-colorable. The boundary may have arbitrary edges and exterior attachments. The proof combines exact coloring relations with ambient planar reductions. An eight-clique behind four roots, with private degrees allowed to range from nine to eleven, has only balanced two-pair extension obstructions; a simultaneous replacement excludes all of them. Abstract completion of a failed rainbow boundary isolates three root roles in $K_7\\vee C_5$. For the two new attachment cases, tight planar component bounds and cyclic vertex links force either a $K_{3,3}$ or incompatible overlapping omission sets. A common nonadjacent root pair can then be identified. The final seven-chromatic branch uses a transfer principle: trimming a private seven-clique to an existing reduction excludes its unique failed three-color boundary pattern, after which the original private graph is recolored. We state all imported geometric results and their computational dependencies explicitly. Standard-library controls examine 165,165 contact profiles for the seven-chromatic remainder, alongside exact clique relations, literal positive colorings and tight component splits. The theorem is a local restriction on minimum counterexamples; it supplies neither an unavoidable family nor a resolution of the Earth–Moon problem.",
